 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 3,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "WEST: Ironbridge Gorge and the Severn. EAST: Dudley, Netherton and Birmingham, assuming the road is open. It may not be."
  },
  {
   "x": 8,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "The name arrived in the 19th century, when coal seams, ironworks, foundries and furnaces filled the country west of Birmingham with smoke. The American visitor Elihu Burritt called it 'black by day and red by night'. The seam under Dudley was ten metres thick in places, among the thickest in England. It is a country of small workshops, canals and a famous accent.",
   "plaque": "w55e_black_country",
   "title": "The Black Country"
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "The centre anchor of the Titanic, about fifteen and a half tonnes, was forged at Netherton by Noah Hingley and Sons in 1910 or 1911 and carried to Dudley station behind a team of horses. Netherton made anchors and chain for great ships for generations, and the Black Country's ironmasters led the world in both.",
   "plaque": "w55e_titanic_anchor",
   "title": "The Anchor of the Titanic"
  },
  {
   "x": 24,
   "y": 22,
   "text": "In 1910 the women chainmakers of Cradley Heath went on strike for a minimum wage under the new Trade Boards Act. They worked at hearths in their back sheds, swinging heavy hammers for very little. The trade union leader Mary Macarthur backed them, and after ten weeks the employers gave in. It was one of the first wins for low-paid women workers in Britain.",
   "plaque": "w55e_chainmakers",
   "title": "The Women Chainmakers"
  },
  {
   "x": 14,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "THE CANAL. Birmingham boasts more miles of canal than Venice, and the Black Country has plenty of them too. The Birmingham Canal Navigations were among Britain's busiest waterways in their day."
  },
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "A castle on the hill above Dudley, begun about 1070 as a motte and bailey and rebuilt in stone from the 12th century. A Royalist garrison held it for the king in the Civil War until it surrendered in 1646, and it was made unusable the following year. Today it stands beside Dudley Zoo.",
   "plaque": "w55e_dudley_castle",
   "title": "Dudley Castle"
  },
  {
   "x": 49,
   "y": 21,
   "text": "THE ROAD EAST. Birmingham, the city of a thousand trades, and the Bull Ring. Don't ask the Wardens, they only know the one word."
  },
  {
   "x": 50,
   "y": 27,
   "text": "BRIDGE INSPECTION IN PROGRESS. Please do not walk on the bridge, over the bridge, or in the vicinity of the bridge. Thank you."
  },
  {
   "x": 5,
   "y": 28,
   "text": "LOCAL SPEAKING. A 'bostin' tea is a very good one. Say 'ow bin ya?' and you'll be told. Do not ask about the football."
  }
 ],
